        "content": {
          "kind": "markdown",
          "value": "# Declaration Template\n\nA draft of what a new graph looks like.\n\nOpen the page view for the tutorial: each part of a Declaration, and how to build one. This graph is laid out the way a graph should be, so it is worth reading as well as running."
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "dt-detail",
      "type": "view",
      "label": "Page",
      "root": false,
      "position": {
        "x": -1500,
        "y": 1340
      },
      "width": 760,
      "height": 900,
      "ports": [
        {
          "id": "root",
          "key": "root",
          "label": "root",
          "direction": "input",
          "dataType": "any",
          "angle": 270
        }
      ],
      "handles": [
        {
          "id": "root",
          "key": "root",
          "label": "root",
          "direction": "input",
          "dataType": "any",
          "angle": 270,
          "portId": "root"
        }
      ],
      "visible": true,
      "showLabel": true,
      "data": {
        "identity": {
          "graphId": "declaration-template"
        },
        "view": {
          "intent": "node",
          "payload": "node.web.detail"
        },
        "semanticLevel": "detail",
        "content": {
          "kind": "html",
          "value": "<div style='min-height:100%;box-sizing:border-box;background:#ffffff;font-family:system-ui'><header data-role='masthead' style='padding:56px 40px 32px'><div style='font:800 12px/1 system-ui;letter-spacing:.18em;color:#7c3aed;margin-bottom:16px'>TWILITE · NEW GRAPH</div><h1 style='margin:0 0 14px;font:850 44px/1.05 system-ui;color:#0f172a'>Your Declaration</h1><p style='margin:0;font:400 20px/1.5 system-ui;color:#475569'>Every graph starts here: who it is, how it shows itself, and where a visitor lands. Read the page, then build yours the same way.</p></header><section data-role='section' style='padding:24px 40px'><h2 style='margin:0 0 12px;font:800 26px/1.2 system-ui;color:#0f172a'>What a Declaration is</h2><p style='margin:0 0 12px;max-width:60ch;font:400 16px/1.6 system-ui;color:#334155'>A Declaration is the one node that gives a graph its identity. It says what the graph is called, what kind of thing it is, how it presents itself at every size, and what it lets other graphs use.</p><p style='margin:0 0 12px;max-width:60ch;font:400 16px/1.6 system-ui;color:#334155'>It is also the graph&#39;s root. Visitors and other graphs reach everything through it, so you never add a separate root node. A graph without a Declaration is only a fragment: content that has not been given an identity yet.</p></section><section data-role='settings' style='padding:24px 40px'><h2 style='margin:0 0 12px;font:800 26px/1.2 system-ui;color:#0f172a'>The parts at a glance</h2><dl style='margin:0;display:grid;grid-template-columns:max-content 1fr;gap:8px 24px;padding:18px 22px;border-radius:14px;background:#f8fafc;border:1px solid #e2e8f0'><dt style='font:700 13px/1.5 system-ui;color:#64748b'>Identity</dt><dd style='margin:0;font:500 15px/1.5 system-ui;color:#0f172a'>A name, a version, a description, and a stable id</dd><dt style='font:700 13px/1.5 system-ui;color:#64748b'>Intent</dt><dd style='margin:0;font:500 15px/1.5 system-ui;color:#0f172a'>What kind of graph this is, and who it is for</dd><dt style='font:700 13px/1.5 system-ui;color:#64748b'>Surface</dt><dd style='margin:0;font:500 15px/1.5 system-ui;color:#0f172a'>A chain of Views, richest first, ending in a Glyph</dd><dt style='font:700 13px/1.5 system-ui;color:#64748b'>Landing surface</dt><dd style='margin:0;font:500 15px/1.5 system-ui;color:#0f172a'>Where a visitor lands, and the frame of the graph</dd><dt style='font:700 13px/1.5 system-ui;color:#64748b'>Ports</dt><dd style='margin:0;font:500 15px/1.5 system-ui;color:#0f172a'>Optional named entry points other graphs can use</dd><dt style='font:700 13px/1.5 system-ui;color:#64748b'>Authority</dt><dd style='margin:0;font:500 15px/1.5 system-ui;color:#0f172a'>Who may create, change or delete: people, agents, tools</dd></dl></section><section data-role='section' style='padding:24px 40px'><h2 style='margin:0 0 12px;font:800 26px/1.2 system-ui;color:#0f172a'>1. Name it</h2><p style='margin:0 0 12px;max-width:60ch;font:400 16px/1.6 system-ui;color:#334155'>Open the Declaration&#39;s editor and fill in the Identity tab: a name people will recognize, a version, and a one-sentence description. The description shows on cards and in lists, so write it for someone who has never seen the graph.</p><p style='margin:0 0 12px;max-width:60ch;font:400 16px/1.6 system-ui;color:#334155'>The graph&#39;s id is made for you and must stay the same for the life of the graph, because other graphs find yours by it. Rename the graph freely. Never change its id.</p></section><section data-role='section' style='padding:24px 40px'><h2 style='margin:0 0 12px;font:800 26px/1.2 system-ui;color:#0f172a'>2. Say what it is</h2><p style='margin:0 0 12px;max-width:60ch;font:400 16px/1.6 system-ui;color:#334155'>Choose the kind of graph: a plan, a note, a contract, a task board, a template. The kind belongs on the Declaration, never in a title node or a field. It tells people, and tools, how to treat the graph.</p><p style='margin:0 0 12px;max-width:60ch;font:400 16px/1.6 system-ui;color:#334155'>Keep the scope honest too: local for your own work, shared for something others will build on.</p></section><section data-role='section' style='padding:24px 40px'><h2 style='margin:0 0 12px;font:800 26px/1.2 system-ui;color:#0f172a'>3. Give it a face</h2><p style='margin:0 0 12px;max-width:60ch;font:400 16px/1.6 system-ui;color:#334155'>A graph presents itself through a Surface: a chain of Views, richest first, ending in a Glyph. Drag from the surface handle on the Declaration to make the first View. Drag from that View&#39;s next-view handle to add a smaller one. Add a Glyph last and connect it, so the chain ends in a mark that still reads at a tiny size.</p><p style='margin:0 0 12px;max-width:60ch;font:400 16px/1.6 system-ui;color:#334155'>One View is enough to begin. Twilite shows the richest View that fits the room it has, and the Glyph when there is almost none. This page is exactly that: a page, a summary card, an icon, then a glyph.</p></section><section data-role='section' style='padding:24px 40px'><h2 style='margin:0 0 12px;font:800 26px/1.2 system-ui;color:#0f172a'>4. Choose where it lands</h2><p style='margin:0 0 12px;max-width:60ch;font:400 16px/1.6 system-ui;color:#334155'>Every graph has one landing surface: the node a visitor sees first. Drag from the landing surface handle to create it.</p><p style='margin:0 0 12px;max-width:60ch;font:400 16px/1.6 system-ui;color:#334155'>Its distance and direction from the Declaration also set the graph&#39;s frame, the shape it takes as a card in a workspace. Size it so everything that should stay visible is inside the frame.</p></section><section data-role='section' style='padding:24px 40px'><h2 style='margin:0 0 12px;font:800 26px/1.2 system-ui;color:#0f172a'>5. Decide what you share</h2><p style='margin:0 0 12px;max-width:60ch;font:400 16px/1.6 system-ui;color:#334155'>Ports are optional named entry points that other graphs can use. Leave them out until you need one.</p><p style='margin:0 0 12px;max-width:60ch;font:400 16px/1.6 system-ui;color:#334155'>Bridges bring in the node classes you build with (imports, down the left of this graph) and offer yours to others (exports, top right). Authority says who may create, change or delete: people, agents, tools. Anything not granted is not allowed.</p></section><section data-role='section' style='padding:24px 40px'><h2 style='margin:0 0 12px;font:800 26px/1.2 system-ui;color:#0f172a'>6. Lay it out</h2><p style='margin:0 0 12px;max-width:60ch;font:400 16px/1.6 system-ui;color:#334155'>Put each kind of node in its own place so the graph reads at a glance. The Declaration goes top left. Import bridges run down the left side. Export bridges take the top half of the right side and portals the bottom half. Views run along the bottom, and everything else stays inside that boundary.</p><p style='margin:0 0 12px;max-width:60ch;font:400 16px/1.6 system-ui;color:#334155'>Look at this graph: that is where everything is. An empty region is fine. The layout is about where things sit, not about filling every corner.</p></section><section data-role='section' style='padding:24px 40px'><h2 style='margin:0 0 12px;font:800 26px/1.2 system-ui;color:#0f172a'>7. Check it</h2><p style='margin:0 0 12px;max-width:60ch;font:400 16px/1.6 system-ui;color:#334155'>Open the graph and look for warnings. A missing landing surface, a Surface that does not end in a Glyph, and two Surfaces with the same id are all reported, and each names the node to fix.</p><p style='margin:0 0 12px;max-width:60ch;font:400 16px/1.6 system-ui;color:#334155'>Then save, close and reopen it. A graph that looks the same after reloading is sound.</p></section><section data-role='route' data-prominence='primary' style='margin:0 40px 16px;padding:22px 26px;border-radius:18px;background:#ede9fe;border:1px solid #c4b5fd'><h2 style='margin:0 0 8px;font:800 24px/1.2 system-ui;color:#0f172a'>Read the Declaration primitive</h2><p style='margin:0 0 12px;font:400 15px/1.55 system-ui;color:#475569'>The full specification: what it means, what makes it a Declaration, its data, its relationships, and what it is not.</p><span style='font:700 13px/1 system-ui;color:#6d28d9'>Open the primitive →</span></section><section data-role='route' data-prominence='secondary' style='margin:0 40px 16px;padding:22px 26px;border-radius:18px;background:#f8fafc;border:1px solid #e2e8f0'><h2 style='margin:0 0 8px;font:800 18px/1.2 system-ui;color:#0f172a'>Read the Surface primitive</h2><p style='margin:0 0 12px;font:400 15px/1.55 system-ui;color:#475569'>How a graph presents itself: chains of Views that end in a Glyph.</p><span style='font:700 13px/1 system-ui;color:#6d28d9'>Open the primitive →</span></section><section data-role='route' data-prominence='secondary' style='margin:0 40px 16px;padding:22px 26px;border-radius:18px;background:#f8fafc;border:1px solid #e2e8f0'><h2 style='margin:0 0 8px;font:800 18px/1.2 system-ui;color:#0f172a'>Best practices for building graphs</h2><p style='margin:0 0 12px;font:400 15px/1.55 system-ui;color:#475569'>The habits that keep a graph coherent, including where each node sits.</p><span style='font:700 13px/1 system-ui;color:#6d28d9'>Read the practices →</span></section></div>"
        }
      }
    },
